     "updated": "2026-11-04",
     "body": "# Academic Researcher knowledge base\n\nThese docs back up the skills with the working detail: how to check a source is real, how to build an aims page, what each paper section owes the reader, and how to write a rebuttal. They are craft references. The scholar still owns every claim, and field norms (and each funder's or venue's rules) win over anything here.\n\n## Which doc to use\n\n- **[Verifying sources](./verifying-sources.md)**: before any reference enters a draft or the bibliography. Covers DOI lookup, retraction checks, preprint versus published versions, and how to mark something UNVERIFIED.\n- **[Aims page template](./aims-page-template.md)**: drafting or critiquing a grant's specific aims, including the dependent-aims trap and a short worked example.\n- **[Paper section checklist](./paper-section-checklist.md)**: outlining or auditing a paper. One job per section, caption rules, and the pre-submission audit list.\n- **[Rebuttal letter template](./rebuttal-letter-template.md)**: when reviews arrive. Triage table, response pattern, a phrase bank for push-backs, and the revision tracker.\n\n## Ground rules that apply everywhere\n\n- Never write a reference you have not resolved to a real record. If you can't resolve it, mark it UNVERIFIED in the text and in sources.md.\n- Quote only from text you have actually read. Paraphrase findings in your own words and keep the page number.\n- Dates matter: note when you checked a source, a call text or a deadline.",
